#{[17592186552511 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{1-{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to -1}({x}^{2}-4)}{\\lim_{x\\to -1}(1-{x}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-4}{1-(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555764 :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186558489 :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h)^{3}.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}-(-7{x}^{3})}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-7(x+h)^{3}+7{x}^{3}}{h}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186558508 :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-5{x}^{2}+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5(x+h)^{2}+2-(-5{x}^{2}+2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+2+5{x}^{2}-2)}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{2}-10xh-5{h}^{2}+2+5{x}^{2}-2}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{-10xh-5{h}^{2}}{h}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-10x-5h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\lim_{h\\to 0}(-10x-5h) \\\\  & =-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562447 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{\\left(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562466 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{3}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({(-3{x}^{3})}^{3}\\cdot {(-3{x}^{3})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-27{x}^{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }((-27\\cdot 9){x}^{9+6}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-243\\cdot {x}^{15}) \\\\  & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{15}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-243$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-243{x}^{15})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186562478 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (15{x}^{7})=15{x}^{9}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)$ and $p(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15{x}^{9}}{6{x}^{9}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562488 :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{11}}{(-7{x}^{11})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562499 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{8}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{8}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{8})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186562505 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(3{x}^{2}\\cdot 5{x}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }((3\\cdot 5){x}^{2+7}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(15\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $15$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(15{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186563647 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 5}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3\\underbrace{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563649 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563649 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit $\\lim_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of $\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563652 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the addition rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -7}[\\frac{f(t)}{2}+3g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to -7}\\frac{f(t)}{2}+\\lim_{t\\to -7}3g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to -7}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\lim_{t\\to -7}f(t)+3\\lim_{t\\to -7}g(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}f(-7)+3g(-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨6,0,-2⟩+3⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-1⟩+⟨6,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563660 :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}}) & =\\lim_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\lim_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{(\\lim_{t\\to -1}(2t+1))}^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{{(\\lim_{t\\to -1}({t}^{2}+t+1))}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(2(-1)+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(((-1)^{2}+(-1)+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{((1-1+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{-1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563661 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$"] [17592186563662 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5} & =\\frac{4+1}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{(t-1)(t+1)} \\\\  & =(\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot (\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{1+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t} & =\\sqrt{9(1)}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)=i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $1$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563663 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563663 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563664 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563664 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of $\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563667 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & =\\lim_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563676 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={(\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563690 :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-\\underbrace{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563691 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}u(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) \\\\  & =u(0)f(0) \\\\  & =3f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)]=⟨-18,-21,6⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[u(t)f(t)] & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ 3f(0) & =⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-18,-21,6⟩ \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-6,-7,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563693 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563706 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563706 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}=\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}=\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563712 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563714 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$"] [17592186563716 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$"] [17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖\\lim_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{bmatrix}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563727 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563727 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563729 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563729 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563738 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$"] [17592186563742 :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}=\\frac{\\lim_{x\\to 2}3x}{\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}3x & =3\\lim_{x\\to 2}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\lim_{x\\to 2}(2{x}^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2(2)^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}} & =\\frac{6}{3}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563751 :question/problem "Find the limit $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563751 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563752 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+(\\lim_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖j+4\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563754 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ \\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186564486 :question/worked-solution "The function ${log}_{2}⁡(4x)$ is well defined for $x>0,$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=4$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{log}_{2}⁡(4x) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡(16) \\\\  & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{4}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186564525 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x}$ is well defined for $x>-4,$ and $x\\ne 2$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=1$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+8)}{2-x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(1)+8)}{2-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(10)}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565411 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186565412 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565415 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186565424 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186565425 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186565432 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186565438 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565443 :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\frac{3}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa92c457186fbdf71552ec79c782af7983e6fa09009e7f0efdd02b8f74e3ab98.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to ({0}^{+})}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty \n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186565463 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"] [17592186565476 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}\\right) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186565477 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186565484 :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$"] [17592186565485 :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186569964 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186569981 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right){cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186570016 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{12{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{12{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta ){\\cancel{cos}}^{2}(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\cancel{sin}\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 0-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/32/32a586a1fe160a6c3a2cfee3cfb78af3d596679cf40abedeb0b8d622a3cf736c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186570042 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)({cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/f4/f4609e6433a861ef255cbeeac60c8a316e5c14a89df45030e26e526e3a1253a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186570702 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{4}}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{8-4\\sqrt{x}}{x-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})}{x-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(2-\\sqrt{x})(2+\\sqrt{x})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4({2}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2})}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{4(4-x)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4(x-4)}{(x-4)(2+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2+\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186570720 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{3(1)+2}-\\sqrt{4(1)+1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2}-\\sqrt{4x+1})(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(\\sqrt{3x+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{4x+1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{(3x+2)-(4x+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-(1-x)(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})}{1-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}[-(\\sqrt{3x+2}+\\sqrt{4x+1})] \\\\  & =-(\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186570736 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3(3)}}{3-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{(3-\\sqrt{3x})(3+\\sqrt{3x})}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{{3}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{9-3x}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3(x-3)}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186570745 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{1}^{2}}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186572665 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-2(-1)-3}{(-1)^{2}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{(x-1)(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x-3}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x-3}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-3}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186572697 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & =\\frac{{3}^{3}-9(3)}{{3}^{2}-5(3)+6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & =\\frac{y({y}^{2}-9)}{(y-2)(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(y-3)(y+3)}{(y-2)(y-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(y+3)}{(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to 3}\\frac{{y}^{3}-9y}{{y}^{2}-5y+6} & =\\lim_{y\\to 3}\\frac{y(y+3)}{y-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3+3)}{3-2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186572728 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{9(0)^{3}-4(0)}{3(0)^{2}+2(0)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\frac{x(9{x}^{2}-4)}{x(3x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{3}-4x}{3{x}^{2}+2x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{9{x}^{2}-4}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-4}{0+2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573250 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi :$   \n\n![](images/42/42eb38a11d9d63e05c0078bae4c052566b3162571e8563f22cd52ae31bc6dbd7.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=4$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573256 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $n\\to 0,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}=e\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{4/7n} & =\\lim_{n\\to 0}{[{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to 0}{(1+n)}^{1/n}]}^{4/7} \\\\  & ={e}^{4/7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573260 :question/worked-solution "First, we let \n\n$$\nL=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{a+h}-\\frac{1}{a})}{h}=L.\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(a).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand so,  $L=-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}.$\n\nFinally, since $L=-\\frac{1}{4},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =4 \\\\ a & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573268 :question/worked-solution "![](images/86/86c0cf22de78e3b773543680c2ace077f97b66ae822436d637c5444903e2330f.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}cos\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)) & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})) \\\\  & =cos\\,(arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573868 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-2{x}^{8})=-2{x}^{10}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)$ and $s(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-22 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186573882 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(2x\\cdot 7{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }((2\\cdot 7){x}^{1+4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(14\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $14$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5} &=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(14{x}^{5})=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186573934 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{5}\\cdot (4x)=4{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions $p(x)$ and ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{6}}{4{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186575527 :question/worked-solution "Applying the root rule, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+6x+9)^{3}} & =\\sqrt{\\lim_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}+6x+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\lim_{x\\to 0}(3{x}^{2}+6x+9))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3(0)^{2}+6(0)+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0+0+9)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{729} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186575559 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-3$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\sqrt[3]{7x-6} & =\\sqrt[3]{7(-3)-6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-27} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186575607 :question/worked-solution "The function $\\sqrt{12{x}^{2}+1}$ is well defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and so to find this limit we substitute $x=2$ into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{12{x}^{2}+1} & =\\sqrt{12\\cdot {2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186575610 :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=-3$ into the expression, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}\\sqrt[3]{3x+9} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(-3)+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186580285 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{g\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x))}^{′}}{(g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x))}^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186580288 :question/worked-solution "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(2), \\\\ \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of two line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $x<2$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.5,$\n\n- for $x>2$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=1.5.$\n\n The derivative does not exist at $x=2.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =0.5, \\\\ \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} & =\\text{DNE}, \\\\ \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186581663 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to \\,-4}\\frac{{y}^{2}+y-12}{{y}^{2}+4y} & =\\frac{(-4)^{2}+(-4)-12}{(-4)^{2}+4(-4)}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}+y-12}{{y}^{2}+4y} & =\\frac{(y+4)(y-3)}{y(y+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-3}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{y\\to \\,-4}\\frac{{y}^{2}+y-12}{{y}^{2}+4y} & =\\lim_{y\\to \\,-4}\\frac{y-3}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-3}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582345 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x).$\n\n![](images/eb/eb99763da194e2e21ccb8032f049be9deb2f94605b70878968ccda1bc093c04c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186582364 :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582364 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2x-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2-{x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186582365 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582368 :question/problem "Find the right-sided limit at $x=32$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 32 \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582368 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the right, we have $x>32,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}\\sqrt[5]{x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{32} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582369 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /4)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582371 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /6)^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582373 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582373 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=0$ from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe right-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (0,1).$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186582382 :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).$\n\n![](images/4e/4e4ca7af674b7747ca14a8ba08fb9bcb33d99950ba3ac9124ded9bd736847c54.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\infty\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186582387 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1<x<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $k?$"] [17592186582387 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+3(1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{1\\cdot \\pi }{4}\\right)+k(1) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+k \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =5 \\\\ k & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186582389 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi }\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
